maybeToNullable :: forall a. Maybe a -> Nullable a
  ! Wrapper around Data.Nullable.toNullable; named for use-site clarity in the WriteForeign instances that need to emit JSON null for Nothing fields.
  ! Used pervasively in writeForeignTransaction and writeForeignPaymentTransaction to convert Maybe fields to Nullable before serialization.
  ~ no callers in scanned roots

%decision DualCaseFormatForReadForeignInstances
  what:    ReadForeign instances accept BOTH PascalCase and SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE; WriteForeign emits only PascalCase; Show emits SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE
  why:     The backend's wire format historically used SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE; a migration to PascalCase happened (or is happening) and both forms exist in storage and on the wire. Accepting both forms in ReadForeign means the frontend doesn't break against either old or new backend builds. WriteForeign emits only the new PascalCase format; the SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE branch is purely for reading legacy data. Show is INCONSISTENT with WriteForeign: showTransactionStatus emits 'CREATED' / 'IN_PROGRESS' for log readability, while writeForeignTransactionStatus emits 'Created' / 'InProgress' for the wire. Two output formats per type, deliberately, because logs and wire have different conventions. PaymentMethod has a special case: the 'Other s' constructor serializes as 'Other:s' (colon-prefixed); the readForeign side strips the prefix and falls back to Other <whole-string> on no match (never fails parsing). Applies to all readForeign*/writeForeign*/show* instances for TransactionStatus, PaymentMethod, TaxCategory, TransactionType, LedgerEntryType, AccountType. When the backend migration completes and no SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE values remain in the wild, the legacy ReadForeign branches can be deleted. Affects is empty here because chase's drift checker validates invariant attachment against signatures only; instance dictionary names live in chaseDataDecls which is not drift-checked.
  affects: 

%decision DiscreteMoneyUSDForAllAmounts
  what:    All Transaction, TransactionItem, PaymentTransaction, LedgerEntry money fields use DiscreteMoney USD
  why:     DiscreteMoney is a phantom-currency-tagged Discrete (Int cents) wrapper from purescript-money. Prevents accidental mixing of currencies and accidental Number arithmetic. Wire format serializes as a plain Int (cents); the WriteForeign instance unwraps to Int.
  affects: 

%open_issue StatusParsingIsLenient
  what:     The ReadForeign instances accept more than one spelling per constructor (for example both "Refunded" and "REFUNDED"), while WriteForeign writes "Refunded" and Show gives "REFUNDED"
  why:      A lenient reader hides drift between the two code bases. The backend parsers are lenient in the same way. Wanted: one spelling per value on the wire, pinned by golden JSON fixtures shared by the Haskell and PureScript suites. Those fixtures do not exist yet.


